# Chapter 1: The Letter. Written from the beats on a blank page by ticket
# 0737 (the story-writing skill, "Rewrites start on a blank page"), after
# 0707's script and the 0724 and 0728 reworks were set aside.
# Beat sheet: docs/story/chapters/ch01.md. Continuity: docs/story/ledger.md.
# Voice cards: docs/story/characters/*.md.
#
# Scene ids, as chapters/ch01.ron and battles/ch01.ron wire them (0803):
#
#   Before the battle (intro_scenes, in this order)
#     ch01_intro                 the opening captions, then the letter
#     ch01_prebattle             the Red Company; the party gathers
#
#   In the battle (triggers, each once)
#     ch01_first_turn            TurnStart(turn: 1, phase: Player)
#     ch01_boss_engage_lead      CombatStart(unit: "red_captain", against: Some("lead"))
#     ch01_boss_engage_sergeant  CombatStart(unit: "red_captain", against: Some("sergeant"))
#     ch01_boss_engage           CombatStart(unit: "red_captain"): anyone else
#     ch01_boss_half             HalfHp(unit: "red_captain")
#     ch01_death_red_captain     UnitFell(unit: "red_captain")
#     ch01_death_lead            UnitFell(unit: "lead"): game over in both modes
#     ch01_death_<id>            UnitFell(unit: "<id>", mode: Some(Classic))
#     ch01_retreat_<id>          UnitFell(unit: "<id>", mode: Some(Casual))
#       for <id> = retainer, sergeant, poacher, keeper, heretic
#
#   After the battle (victory_scenes, in this order)
#     ch01_victory
#     ch01_tbc
#
# The lead has three reply choices: one each in ch01_intro, ch01_prebattle
# and ch01_victory. The objective is Defeat Harl (0845): Tamsin says the
# rest of the company runs when he falls, and no line counts the enemy
# (the map shows them).
#
# The title cinematic (assets/cinematics/title.ron) shows the first three
# boxes of ch01_intro (the opening captions) and the first box of
# ch01_prebattle (Tamsin's news). Keep those boxes first.
#
# Tone (docs/design/setting-and-tone.md, "Where the jokes go"; the
# story-writing skill, Craft): the opening and the battle are played
# straight, which means no comedy bits, not no warmth. The care is on the
# body (a shoulder, wet clothes, bread, a hand). The lead's wry reply may
# get a light answer, briefly. Punctuation is the voice acting: Tamsin and
# Harl raise their voices, Maud breaks off, Aske ends flat, Hollis trails
# off once a scene at most.
#
# Expressions: the plain build has no portrait for anyone but the lead
# (the cast's are bought art, assets-private/), so this script uses only
# the five every character has: neutral, happy, angry, sad, surprised.
#
# A companion who fell in Classic is gone (0716; @if blocks, 0715,
# ADR-0055). ch01_victory plays without any of the five, and without all
# of them: the order's seal, that only the new lord may use it, and the
# road to the hall reach the player on every path. Read it with one
# companion gone, and with only the lead left, before changing it.
# docs/story/ledger.md ("If a companion died in Chapter 1") records what
# each death leaves unsaid.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Before the battle
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The yard behind the rented house, at dawn (harrowby_yard.map). Hollis
# has just lost a bout for the first time in four years, and has had the
# letter in his coat since the peddler came. The lead says the name; he
# won't. He ends on porridge, boots and an order about the fire.

@scene ch01_intro
@music village_home
> Three years after {n:event.ash_peace}. The war is over. The soldiers who fought it were never paid.
> Some went home. Some are still on the roads, in their old companies, under their old captains.
> In {n:place.thornmarch}, at the far edge of the kingdom, an exile is up before dawn with a wooden sword.
@caption {N:place.harrowby}, dawn
@place harrowby_yard 12,8
@opening ch01_sparring
@left lead neutral
@right retainer neutral
retainer: That's good. No-- don't help me up, I'm getting up. That was good, kid. You dropped your shoulder after, though. Don't drop it after.
> He sits down on the chopping block to get his breath, and his hand goes into his coat and comes out with a letter. It has been folded and unfolded more than once.
retainer: Peddler brought it last night, after you'd gone to bed. I should have woken you. ...I didn't.
> No name on it. "Get out of the march. Tonight. They are coming for everyone who was there that night. Burn this."
lead: {n:rival.first}.
retainer[sad]: Aye. That's the boy's hand. I sat behind it while it learned its letters, same table as yours.
retainer: Everyone who was there. Four of us, at that mill. My girl's dead. So that's you and me, and him, writing letters.
@choice
* earnest: He's still looking out for us, {n:retainer.first}.
  retainer[sad]: Is he. Four years, and that's what he sends. Not "how are you". Not "how's my father".
* wry: Four years, and this is the letter I get.
  retainer: Hm. That's him, start to finish. Never wrote a line in his life that wasn't an order.
* blunt: Who's "they"?
  retainer[angry]: If I knew that I'd be saddling the horse, not sitting on a block in the cold.
@endchoice
> He takes the letter back and puts it inside his coat, nowhere near the fire.
retainer[neutral]: Eat something. Then boots, the good ones. And nobody is burning anything in this house.
@end

# By the chapel, minutes later (ch01.map, where the party starts). Tamsin
# talks down from the saddle; Maud gives herself away and nobody asks;
# Rue feeds the wet girl; Hollis says nothing until the swords are out.

@scene ch01_prebattle
@caption {N:place.harrowby}, by the chapel
@place battle 3,12
@music dungeon_tense
@left lead neutral
@right sergeant angry
sergeant: {n:red_captain.nickname}'s in the toll-fort, Your Former Grace! Took it in the night. I rode up to the gate like a fool and he leaned over the wall and asked if I wanted my old job back.
sergeant: He had a paper. A lord's seal on it, and his thumb over the seal the whole time. "The exile. The old man. The girl with the burned hands." A price by each. He read it to me slow.
@left keeper neutral
keeper[surprised]: The girl with the burned-- how do they know about her hands? Who would have-- no. No, go on, I'm sorry. Go on.
@right heretic angry
heretic: You can all stop looking at my hands. They're hands. Is the heir on that list? Good. Then it isn't only me.
@right poacher neutral
poacher: They shoot badly. I lay under their wall all night. A hundred arrows for three deer, from a wall, and they took one and left two for the crows, noble.
@right heretic neutral
heretic: You. Eat this. You're gray in the face, and if you fall over before they've even shot at us I'm not carrying you.
@right poacher angry
poacher: I am not cold. I am angry. They were good deer, and I want those men out of my woods. Half. You eat half, you are thinner than me.
@right heretic angry
keeper: {n:heretic}. The chapel cellar. It's dry, and nobody would think to-- I have buried four children from this village and I am not burying you. Go down. Please.
heretic: I've had enough of cellars, Keeper. I'm sworn to fire, I can burn all of them-- I can burn some of them. I'm not sitting in a hole while you all die over my hands.
@right retainer neutral
> {n:retainer.first} comes back out of the house with a blanket roll under his arm and unrolls it on the grass. Two swords, oiled, from under the floor.
retainer: Pick one. Then it's your call, {n:family.veyne}. What are we doing?
@choice
* earnest: Nobody in {n:place.harrowby} pays for this but us.
  @right poacher surprised
  poacher: Good. That is good. The miller's wife has a bad hip. She cannot run.
  @right retainer neutral
* wry: A lord's seal, and they didn't even use my name.
  @right sergeant happy
  sergeant: Ha! Did you hear that, Captain? Did you hear what-- right, enough, I'm shutting up. Who's got a spare girth, mine's rotten through.
  @right retainer neutral
* blunt: We go to the fort and we kill him.
  @right heretic angry
  heretic: Oh, now the heir wants a fight? Good. You first, though. I want you in front of me.
  @right retainer neutral
@endchoice
@right sergeant neutral
sergeant: It's {n:red_captain.first}. Get him and you've got all of them, those men are his, not the paper's, and the minute he's down they're a mob nobody's paying and they'll run. I've watched them run.
> {lead} picks up the nearer sword. {n:retainer.first} takes the other.
@end

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# In the battle
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

@scene ch01_first_turn
@left retainer neutral
retainer: Stay by me, kid. Let them come down the road to us. And keep that shoulder up.
@end

# Harl's first fight. A scene written for him and one character replaces
# the general one (docs/design/battle-scenes-and-recruitment.md).

@scene ch01_boss_engage
@right red_captain neutral
red_captain: You're not on my paper. Nobody's paying for you, so what's this, charity? Go home. I've got men to feed.
@end

@scene ch01_boss_engage_lead
@left lead neutral
@right red_captain neutral
red_captain: Your Grace. Three hundred crowns a head, you are, and I'd have done it for less. Who's paying? Come and take the paper off me, and read it.
@end

@scene ch01_boss_engage_sergeant
@left sergeant angry
@right red_captain neutral
red_captain: {n:sergeant.last}. Look at you. Fat on village bread while the rest of us ate the horses.
sergeant: You burned a farm for a lord's purse, {n:red_captain.first}. Don't you talk to me about hungry!
red_captain[angry]: I brought forty men home from that war. Forty! And the crown gave us the road to starve on. You held the horses at that farm, {n:sergeant.last}. Don't stand there like you didn't.
sergeant[sad]: ...I know what I held.
@end

@scene ch01_boss_half
@right red_captain angry
red_captain: Ah-- that's-- no. Not here, not for a-- Come on, then! I'm not done yet.
@end

# Said to the lead, who may be nowhere near him. He hands over the one
# thing he knows (the seal, the silver) because it hurts, then begs for
# his men.

@scene ch01_death_red_captain
@left lead neutral
@right red_captain sad
red_captain: Your Grace... here's a thing to take home. The new lord of {n:family.veyne} paid for you up front, in good silver. Your own crest in the wax, and he never blinked.
red_captain: My boys-- don't let them hang my boys. They were hungry. That's all they ever were, was hungry.
@end

# The lead's fall is game over in both modes.

@scene ch01_death_lead
@left lead sad
> {lead} goes down in the road. Somebody is shouting {their} name, too far away.
@end

# The companions: a death quote in Classic, a retreat line in Casual
# (docs/design/death-and-difficulty.md). Last words are about the living;
# the shapes differ on purpose (an errand, a question, a will, a plea).
# They can fire in any order, so a line about another companion sits in
# an @if block for them (Maud's).

@scene ch01_death_retainer
@left retainer sad
retainer: Don't come over here, kid. Stay in the line. Shoulder up-- that's it. ...The porridge pot's still on the fire at the house. Somebody take it off.
@end

@scene ch01_retreat_retainer
@left retainer angry
retainer: Leg. Close up where I was. Don't look at me, close it up. I'm going back up the road.
@end

@scene ch01_death_sergeant
@left sergeant sad
sergeant: Who's down? Is anybody else down? Tell me-- no, I want to know, tell me who's-- and get the mare out of this, she's never seen blood.
@end

@scene ch01_retreat_sergeant
@left sergeant angry
sergeant: Catch the mare! Get her by the bridle before she's in the river. It's only my arm. The horse, get the horse.
@end

@scene ch01_death_poacher
@left poacher sad
poacher: My left boot. There is silver in it, for my {n:word.mother}. It goes north. Noble. You take it north. You promise me.
@end

@scene ch01_retreat_poacher
@left poacher angry
poacher: The draw arm. Four arrows left in the quiver. Take them, they are good ones. Do not waste them.
@end

@scene ch01_death_keeper
@left keeper sad
@if heretic
keeper: {n:heretic}-- don't let her go in a cellar. Not ever, not for anyone, I-- oh. Oh, I'm not finished, I haven't--
@else
keeper: The children at the chapel-- somebody see that they've eaten, they won't ask. Oh. Oh, I'm not finished, I haven't--
@endif
@end

@scene ch01_retreat_keeper
@left keeper surprised
keeper: Is it bad? Don't tell me, I can-- I can see it's bad. {n:god.mother.name} keep me. All right, I'm going back to the chapel, I know the way.
@end

@scene ch01_death_heretic
@left heretic sad
heretic: Who's going to find the others now? From the barracks, the ones who got out-- not you. Not you, heir. ...Why is it cold? It's never cold, I'm never-- why is it cold?
@end

@scene ch01_retreat_heretic
@left heretic angry
heretic: It won't light. Why won't it light, it always lights! Fine. I'm going behind the wall. I'm not running, I'm going behind the wall.
@end

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# After the battle
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The toll-fort, just below its keep at (20, 2). Tamsin over the body;
# Hollis finds the order and says nothing; Tamsin names the boy; Rue has
# seen the wax before; Maud takes the lead's hidden cut; Aske wants her
# arrows; Hollis names the day's dead and goes for a shovel.

@scene ch01_victory
@caption The toll-fort, noon
@place battle 20,3
@music scene_sad
> One barn in {n:place.harrowby} is still smoking. Inside the fort there is a dead fire, a sack of moldy oats, and {n:red_captain.first}.
@left lead neutral
@if sergeant
@right sergeant neutral
> {n:sergeant.first} kneels by him with the ledger open on her knee and writes. Then she puts her hand over his eyes for a moment.
sergeant[sad]: He put me on my first horse. I fell off it in front of the whole yard and he laughed till he had to sit down. ...It was a job. That's what I'm writing. A job.
@right clear
@else
> {n:red_captain.first} lies where he fell with his eyes open. Nobody closes them.
@endif
@if retainer
> {n:retainer.first} goes through the dead man's coat the way he has gone through a hundred, and stops. A folded paper, sealed in blue wax.
@else
> {lead} goes through the dead man's coat {themself}. A folded paper, sealed in blue wax.
@endif
@if sergeant
@right sergeant angry
@if retainer
sergeant: Captain? Captain, sit down, you're white as paper. ...It's the boy's. Isn't it. Your title, Your Former Grace, and his boy holding it, the hero of the war, and that's his seal.
@else
sergeant: That's your title's seal, Your Former Grace, and the one holding your title is the hero of the war. {n:rival}. The Captain's boy. He never had to see this, at least. There's that.
@endif
@right clear
@else
> In the wax, the crest of {n:house.veyne}, the one {lead} cut off {their} own coat. Only the one who holds the title seals with it, and the title is {n:rival}'s now.
@endif
@if retainer
@right retainer sad
> {n:retainer.first} folds the paper along its folds and puts it in {lead}'s hand without a word. Then he goes out and starts picking up the dead men's spears.
@right clear
@endif
@if heretic
@right heretic angry
heretic: I've seen that wax before, heir. Nailed to a cart, at night. There were children in it. Was it you? ...Was it your father?
@right clear
@endif
@if keeper
@left keeper sad
@if heretic
keeper: We can't stay here. Any of us. {N:faction.vigil} knows she's-- somebody knows she's here, and they'll send more. Give me your hand. No, the other one, the one you've been hiding. Hold still.
@else
keeper: We can't stay here. Any of us. They'll send more, and-- somebody was told she was here. Somebody. Give me your hand. No, the other one, the one you've been hiding. Hold still.
@endif
@left lead neutral
@endif
@if poacher
@right poacher neutral
poacher: Nine of my arrows are in those men and I am taking every one back. And I am coming after, for the rest of them. You would lose them otherwise. It is for the arrows. Why are you smiling.
@right clear
@endif
@if sergeant
@right sergeant neutral
@if poacher
sergeant: I'm not. She's coming for arrows and I'm coming for pay, and anybody who writes it down different can take it up with me. Further than the pay, probably. Has anybody fed my horse?
@else
sergeant: I'm coming for the pay, and anybody who writes it down different can take it up with me. Further than the pay, probably. Don't make a thing of it. Has anybody fed my horse?
@endif
@right clear
@endif
@if retainer
@right retainer neutral
retainer: The boy's at {n:place.veyne_hall}. North, past the boundary stone, and past the stone you're an outlaw. Your call. Not mine.
@choice
* earnest: We go and ask him. To his face.
  retainer[sad]: Aye. Ask him. ...Ask him why he didn't come himself.
* wry: Outlaw. It has a ring to it.
  retainer: Hm. Pack dry socks.
* blunt: {N:place.veyne_hall}. Tonight.
  retainer: Tonight, then.
@endchoice
@if sergeant
@else
retainer[sad]: {n:sergeant.first} owed me two crowns at dice and brought it up every week like I'd forget. Her horse is still standing saddled at the gate. Somebody see to it.
@endif
@if keeper
@else
retainer[sad]: The Keeper couldn't lie and couldn't play dice, and sat down to lose every week anyway. I'll dig hers deep. By the chapel wall.
@endif
@if heretic
@else
retainer[sad]: The girl. Twice this week she took bread off my table and glared at me for noticing. She was next to me on that paper, and I don't know her family to tell them.
@endif
@if poacher
@else
retainer[sad]: {n:poacher} drilled worse than any recruit I ever had, and came back every morning to be told so. She faces north. See she faces north.
@endif
retainer[neutral]: Find me a shovel. I'm not leaving {n:red_captain.first} to the crows. ...There must be one in this place, they dug a latrine.
@right clear
@else
> {n:retainer.first}'s letter is in {lead}'s coat now, out of his. {They} doesn't burn it either. {N:place.veyne_hall} is north, past the boundary stone, and past the stone {they} is an outlaw.
@choice
* earnest: We go and ask him. To his face.
  > Nobody argues.
* wry: Outlaw. It has a ring to it.
  > Nobody laughs.
* blunt: {N:place.veyne_hall}. Tonight.
  > Nobody says otherwise.
@endchoice
> {lead} digs the graves by the chapel {themself}, one by one. Nobody digs one for {n:red_captain.first}.
@endif
@end

# The old lord's study, at night (veyne_study.map). Dace lies to the
# Vigil's messenger and burns the report.

@scene ch01_tbc
@caption {N:place.veyne_hall}
@place veyne_study 12,7
@music talk_antagonist
@opening ch01_pacing
> Night. The old lord's study, the old lord's chair, and a fire somebody else laid. {n:rival.first} reads a report standing up.
@left rival neutral
@right messenger neutral
messenger: My lord. {N:vowmaster.title} asks whether it is done.
> The report says every man of {n:faction.red_company} is dead, and the exile is not.
rival: Tell him it's done. Tell him I said so.
@right clear
> When the door shuts he puts the report in the fire and holds it there with the poker until it is gone. Over the hearth hangs the blue sash, and on it the whole crest.
rival[sad]: I wrote it down for you, {n:family.veyne}. Get out. Tonight. One thing I've asked of you in four years, and you-- Of course you didn't.
@end
